  "query_preview": "..Scattered midlevel clouds today with temperatures \nright around normal. The bigger story is continued breezy to locally \nwindy conditions with a tight surface pressure gradient remaining in \nplace through later on this evening. We'll see a break in the \nenhanced breeziness Tuesday through Wednesday before before winds \nramp back up again Thursday and especially into the upcoming \nweekend. We're generally looking at sustained easterly winds of 15 \nto 25 mph with occasional gusts to between 30 to 35 mph. What this \nequates to is minor nuisance type of impacts. Considering the time \nof year, potential nuisance impacts might include patio furniture \nand holiday decorations being blowing around, so consider securing \nloose objects and keep an eye on the trash cans as well.\n\nOtherwise, warm and dry conditions continue. There is a chance for \nhigh temperatures to nudge over 100 degrees across central Pima \nCounty (10-30%) and western Pima County (30-60%) each afternoon \nthrough Friday. For early October heat of this magnitude results in \nminor to locally moderate HeatRisk affecting individuals who are \nrather sensitive to the heat. Elsewhere, highs will be in the upper \n80s to mid 90s which is still above normal by about 5 to 8 degrees.\n\nSomething else to keep an eye on in the extended forecast is the \nfact that ensembles continue to show a trough approaching the area \nlate this weekend into early next week. However, models are \ndisagreeing on the timing and overall magnitude of the trough. A \ntrough would support rising motion across portions of southeast \nArizona which could result in showers and storms if the timing \naligns with residual moisture from Hurricane Rachel. For now, we \nhave slight chances (20-40%) for showers and storms for portions of \nsoutheast Arizona and we will continue to monitor.",
